<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanghamitta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">282–<a href="/years/203-bce">203 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Saṅghamittā, Sanghamitra, Sangamitta, Ayapali</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sanghamitta was an Indian Buddhist nun who, according to Sri Lankan tradition, was the eldest daughter of the Mauryan emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>. With her brother Mahinda she entered the Buddhist order, and at the request of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devanampiya-tissa">Devanampiya Tissa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> she was sent there with other nuns to ordain Anula and the women of the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a>, founding the Bhikkhuni <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangha">Sangha</a>, the order of nuns. She brought a sapling of the Bodhi tree, which was planted at Anuradhapura and still survives. Tradition holds that she died in 203 BCE at the age of 79. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> Buddhists in Sri Lanka mark her arrival every year on the full moon of December, Unduvapa Poya or Sanghamitta Day.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kings, merchants and guilds gave the orders caves, monasteries and land: Ashoka gave the Ajivikas caves in the Barabar Hills and, by Sri Lankan tradition, sent his son Mahinda and his daughter <b>Sanghamitta</b> to take Buddhism to Sri Lanka.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classical-india">Social Hierarchy of Classical India</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23.18,75.78&amp;year=-231" aria-label="Ujjain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6573 1309 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sanghamitta"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7023 1579h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7023 1579h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=23.18,75.78&amp;year=-231">What was here in 231 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
